1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:02,583
Bonjour. This is Liam, in for Bear.

2
00:00:03,042 --> 00:00:04,750
Here are twenty-five more candidate props.

3
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:07,500
Each one shows a design choice, a recovery
step,

4
00:00:07,667 --> 00:00:08,458
or a learning action.

5
00:00:09,083 --> 00:00:11,292
Judge what the motion explains, not just
how the

6
00:00:11,292 --> 00:00:11,958
icon looks.

7
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:14,750
Three terms. A control changes a setting.

8
00:00:15,208 --> 00:00:17,375
Recovery returns work to a usable state.

9
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:18,958
Evidence supports a judgment.

10
00:00:19,458 --> 00:00:22,250
These drawings are teaching examples, not
reports of tests

11
00:00:22,250 --> 00:00:22,875
we have run.

12
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:25,583
A suggestion offers a starting point.

13
00:00:25,917 --> 00:00:28,542
Move the proposed instruction toward the
arrow, then leave

14
00:00:28,542 --> 00:00:29,708
room to revise it.

15
00:00:30,417 --> 00:00:31,833
Suggested does not mean required.

16
00:00:32,625 --> 00:00:35,583
Clarification sends a question back before
work continues.

17
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:37,083
Separate the two directions.

18
00:00:37,625 --> 00:00:40,417
Use this when missing context matters more
than producing

19
00:00:40,417 --> 00:00:41,417
an immediate answer.

20
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:44,042
A token can stand for a unit in a

21
00:00:44,042 --> 00:00:46,375
budget. Separate one segment from the
rest.

22
00:00:46,708 --> 00:00:49,208
This is a teaching metaphor, not a literal
drawing

23
00:00:49,208 --> 00:00:50,583
of language tokenization.

24
00:00:51,375 --> 00:00:53,792
A filter narrows a collection according to
a rule.

25
00:00:54,208 --> 00:00:56,000
Move a record past successive levels.

26
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:58,917
The rule must be stated; filtering alone
does not

27
00:00:58,917 --> 00:01:00,250
make information trustworthy.

28
00:01:01,125 --> 00:01:03,542
Sorting changes order without changing the
collection.

29
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,625
Swap the short and long bars.

30
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,042
Name the ordering rule before interpreting
whichever item ends

31
00:01:09,042 --> 00:01:09,500
up first.

32
00:01:10,375 --> 00:01:13,208
An adaptive form asks a follow-up question
only when

33
00:01:13,208 --> 00:01:16,083
it applies. Reveal the second field after
the first

34
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:19,083
choice. More fields are not automatically
more useful.

35
00:01:19,875 --> 00:01:23,083
Responsive design preserves the task
across different screens.

36
00:01:23,542 --> 00:01:25,458
Separate the desktop, tablet, and phone.

37
00:01:25,917 --> 00:01:28,583
The layout can change while the essential
decision stays

38
00:01:28,583 --> 00:01:29,083
available.

39
00:01:29,875 --> 00:01:31,708
A slider makes a range adjustable.

40
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:33,625
Move the handle along its track.

41
00:01:34,083 --> 00:01:36,583
Name what changes and provide a way to
understand

42
00:01:36,583 --> 00:01:39,042
the selected value without guessing from
position.

43
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,875
A toggle expresses a two-state choice.

44
00:01:42,292 --> 00:01:43,792
Move it from one end to the other.

45
00:01:44,083 --> 00:01:46,750
For a consequential action, make the
current state clear

46
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:47,875
before anything happens.

47
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:51,625
Accessibility is support for completing a
task, not a

48
00:01:51,625 --> 00:01:54,583
decorative badge. Bring the support into
place beneath the

49
00:01:54,583 --> 00:01:57,458
figure. Then test the real experience with
its users.

50
00:01:58,292 --> 00:02:01,042
Keyboard focus shows where the next action
will land.

51
00:02:01,292 --> 00:02:03,167
Move the focus marker between controls.

52
00:02:03,625 --> 00:02:06,708
A visible ring should track navigation,
not remain around

53
00:02:06,708 --> 00:02:07,500
the first item.

54
00:02:08,292 --> 00:02:10,083
A data warning interrupts an assumption.

55
00:02:10,542 --> 00:02:12,917
Reveal the warning, then pull out the
affected record.

56
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:16,208
The next step is investigation, not
turning the warning

57
00:02:16,208 --> 00:02:17,458
into a reassuring check.

58
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:20,750
A failed exchange should stop the next
handoff.

59
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:23,500
Bring the outgoing record to the broken
connection and

60
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,417
hold it. Do not show movement that
suggests delivery

61
00:02:26,417 --> 00:02:26,958
succeeded.

62
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:30,250
Undo returns from a recent action to its
earlier

63
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:32,417
state. Move the same record back.

64
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:35,083
Keep it distinct from restoring an older
backup, which

65
00:02:35,083 --> 00:02:36,208
may replace more work.

66
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:38,750
Restore recovers a saved state.

67
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:41,125
Bring the snapshot back into the recovery
ring.

68
00:02:41,417 --> 00:02:44,250
Name which snapshot you used, and verify
what changed

69
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:45,375
after the restoration.

70
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:49,292
Release history connects a deployed
package to earlier decisions.

71
00:02:49,708 --> 00:02:52,042
Move the clock beside the package, then
reveal a

72
00:02:52,042 --> 00:02:55,208
record. A timestamp alone cannot explain
why the release

73
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:55,750
changed.

74
00:02:56,583 --> 00:02:58,167
A work board makes status visible.

75
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:00,083
Move one task between columns.

76
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:03,167
Define what the destination means;
arriving in a done

77
00:03:03,167 --> 00:03:05,875
column is not evidence that the work
passed review.

78
00:03:06,708 --> 00:03:08,208
A timeline preserves sequence.

79
00:03:08,667 --> 00:03:09,833
Follow the events in order.

80
00:03:10,292 --> 00:03:12,833
This schematic has no time scale, so the
distances

81
00:03:12,833 --> 00:03:15,333
between events do not claim how long
anything took.

82
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:17,417
A metric needs a question.

83
00:03:17,792 --> 00:03:20,083
Move the lens toward the change in this
illustrative

84
00:03:20,083 --> 00:03:23,000
trend. Ask what was measured and what
evidence could

85
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,500
support a different interpretation.

86
00:03:25,417 --> 00:03:27,083
A table keeps records comparable.

87
00:03:27,542 --> 00:03:29,875
Pull one row aside while the other rows
remain.

88
00:03:30,375 --> 00:03:32,625
Use this when the lesson depends on
inspecting an

89
00:03:32,625 --> 00:03:34,208
individual case within a collection.

90
00:03:35,042 --> 00:03:37,292
A quiz asks the learner to try before
seeing

91
00:03:37,292 --> 00:03:40,042
the answer. Move the question aside and
reveal a

92
00:03:40,042 --> 00:03:43,083
response card. The teaching value comes
from the attempt

93
00:03:43,083 --> 00:03:43,667
and feedback.

94
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,083
Reference reading connects a person to
material they can

95
00:03:47,083 --> 00:03:49,625
inspect. Open the two pages around the
reader.

96
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,250
Show the source itself when the lesson
turns on

97
00:03:52,250 --> 00:03:53,125
its exact wording.

98
00:03:53,917 --> 00:03:56,792
An experiment changes something so its
consequences can be

99
00:03:56,792 --> 00:03:59,500
examined. Lower the instrument toward the
sample.

100
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:02,208
This prop represents a test; it does not
provide

101
00:04:02,208 --> 00:04:03,167
a test result.

102
00:04:03,917 --> 00:04:07,417
A map reveals relationships that one
isolated location cannot

103
00:04:07,417 --> 00:04:09,458
show. Unfold the three panels.

104
00:04:09,958 --> 00:04:12,583
For a workflow map, label the places
people actually

105
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:13,333
need to find.

106
00:04:14,083 --> 00:04:16,417
A milestone marks a condition worth
checking.

107
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,333
Raise the flag after the work reaches that
point.

108
00:04:19,708 --> 00:04:21,833
Keep the milestone separate from a claim
that the

109
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:23,042
whole project is complete.

110
00:04:23,792 --> 00:04:27,292
These twenty-five candidates are
simplified adaptations of Isocons,

111
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:30,708
